# Local development environment template.
#
# You usually do NOT need a .env file locally. `bun run dev` works with zero
# configuration: it defaults to SQLite at .tmp/dev.db and skips Docker entirely.
#
# Copy this file to .env (or export the variables in your shell) ONLY if you
# want to override the defaults below.

PORT=3001

# Comma-separated CIDRs for reverse proxies whose X-Forwarded-For header may
# be trusted. Leave empty for direct/local development.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=127.0.0.1/32,::1/128

# ─── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Default: SQLite at .tmp/dev.db (no Docker, no Postgres process).
# Set DATABASE_URL only if you want to use Postgres or a custom SQLite path.
#
#   SQLite (file):    DATABASE_URL=sqlite:./path/to/your.db
#   SQLite (memory):  DATABASE_URL=sqlite::memory:
#   Postgres (auto):  DATABASE_URL=postgres://instatic:instatic@127.0.0.1:5433/instatic
#                     (`bun run dev` will start the Docker postgres service automatically)
#
# DATABASE_URL=sqlite:./.tmp/dev.db

# ─── Filesystem paths ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
UPLOADS_DIR=./uploads
STATIC_DIR=./dist

# ─── AI credential encryption ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local dev auto-creates .tmp/secret.key. Production deployments must set
# ECOBUILDER_SECRET_KEY to the output of:
#
#   bun run scripts/generate-secret-key.ts
#
# ECOBUILDER_SECRET_KEY=

# ─── Email delivery (signup verification, password reset, invitations) ───────
# One sendEmail() abstraction (server/email/). With no provider configured it
# logs the actionable link to the console — signup/verify/reset work in dev and
# CI without any provider or secret. Set EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend + RESEND_API_KEY
# to send real mail.
# EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend
# EMAIL_FROM=Ecobuilder <no-reply@ecobuilder.ai>
# RESEND_API_KEY=

# ─── Social sign-in (Google / GitHub OAuth) ──────────────────────────────────
# A provider's button appears on the sign-in screen only while BOTH its vars
# are set — nothing else to toggle, and no redeploy needed. Register the
# callback <public-origin>/admin/api/cms/auth/oauth/<provider>/callback in the
# provider's OAuth app first (GitHub allows one callback per app, so dev needs
# a separate app pointing at localhost).
# OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OAUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# OAUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ─── Self-service signup ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Public signup (POST /admin/api/cms/signup) creates a user + their own
# workspace. Leave SAAS_MODE unset for a pure self-hosted single-tenant install;
# set SAAS_MODE=1 for the hosted multi-tenant product. (Gate wiring lands with
# the signup UI; the endpoint already exists.)
# SAAS_MODE=1

# ─── Billing (Stripe — per-workspace subscriptions) ──────────────────────────
# Billing is per workspace (tenant): checkout carries the tenant id as the
# reference, a local `subscriptions` table is synced only by signature-verified
# webhooks, and feature gating reads that local table. Sandbox/test keys are
# fine; real keys are NOT required for the app to run or for tests (webhooks are
# exercised with signed mock payloads).
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# STRIPE_PRICE_PRO_MONTHLY=
# STRIPE_PRICE_PRO_YEARLY=
